### Event Listeners vs. Adapter Classes in Java

- **Listener Interface:** An interface defining event handler callback methods (e.g. `MouseListener` has 5 methods: `mouseClicked`, `mousePressed`, `mouseReleased`, `mouseEntered`, `mouseExited`). A class implementing this interface is **forced to override all 5 methods**, creating bloated empty methods.
- **Adapter Class:** An abstract class that provides empty default implementations for all methods in a listener interface (e.g. `MouseAdapter`). Developers extend the adapter and override **only the specific method needed**.

```java
// Extending Adapter: Only override mouseClicked!
panel.addMouseListener(new MouseAdapter() {
    public void mouseClicked(MouseEvent e) {
        System.out.println("Clicked at: " + e.getX() + ", " + e.getY());
    }
});
```

$$\therefore \text{Adapter classes eliminate empty boilerplate methods when handling multi-method event interfaces.}$$
